<BaseLayout title="Colophon — Quiet Compass" description="The tools, design principles, and evidence boundaries behind Quiet Compass."><section><div class="shell narrow prose"><span class="route">Colophon / Behind the experience</span><h1>Quiet on purpose.</h1><p class="lead">A static-first portfolio with expressive typography, clear routes, and interactions that explain the work.</p><h2>The visual system</h2><p>Fraunces provides the editorial voice; Inter handles the interface. Cream, ink, and restrained botanical accents make room for the project artifacts. Dark mode uses the same hierarchy instead of becoming a different identity.</p><h2>The implementation</h2><p>Astro builds the pages. Pagefind indexes the work and essays. Responsive AVIF and WebP images present original concept interfaces. JavaScript enhances search, comparisons, filters, and recommendations; reading and ordinary navigation do not depend on it.</p><h2>What the portfolio demonstrates</h2><p>Atlas, Fieldnote, and Threshold are concept studies. Their starting states and redesigned states were created for these explorations. Their counts describe the artifacts, not measured client outcomes. No testimonials or research participants are invented.</p><h2>What remains a separate validation step</h2><p>Automated browser checks are not a substitute for representative-user evaluation, physical iPhone testing, or assistive-technology review. Real-user performance requires sufficient field data. No 9/10 score or accessibility certification is claimed merely because the site builds.</p><h2>The next step</h2><p>The contact page states whether live delivery is configured. Until it is, the local draft tools let you prepare a brief without pretending a message has been sent.</p><a class="btn" href={withBase('privacy/')}>Privacy & accessibility →</a></div></section></BaseLayout>
